o "},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ask your pharmacist."},{"type":"completed","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"opaque","provider":"anthropic","kind":"thinking","data":{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"}},{"type":"text","text":"Yes, for most healthy adults it's generally safe to take ibuprofen and acetaminophen (paracetamol) together. They work differently and don't interact with each other. You can take them at the same time or alternate them. Some doctors recommend combining them for pain such as dental pain or post-injury pain, because the combination can work better than either one alone.\n\n**Tips**\n- **Stay within the daily limit for each drug.** Check the label. For adults, the usual maximums are about 3,000–4,000 mg a day for acetaminophen and 1,200 mg a day for over-the-counter ibuprofen. A doctor may prescribe more for ibuprofen.\n- **Watch for hidden acetaminophen.** Many cold, flu and sleep products contain it, so check those labels to avoid doubling up.\n- **Take ibuprofen with food or milk** to reduce stomach irritation.\n- **Use the lowest dose for the shortest time that works.**\n\n**Check with a doctor or pharmacist first if you:**\n- Have kidney disease, liver disease, heart disease, high blood pressure, or a history of stomach ulcers or GI bleeding\n- Are pregnant or breastfeeding (ibuprofen is generally avoided in later pregnancy)\n- Take blood thinners, other NSAIDs (aspirin, naproxen), certain blood pressure drugs (ACE inhibitors, diuretics), or drink alcohol heavily\n- Are giving it to a child. Dosing is weight-based, so follow pediatric guidance.\n\nSee a doctor if the pain lasts more than a few days (10 days for pain, 3 days for fever) or gets worse.\n\nThis is general information, not personal medical advice. If you tell me your age, health conditions and other medications, I can point out things to ask your pharmacist."}]},"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":644,"cache_read_input_